  "text_md": "(4) The privilege of out cattle on commons. North. turning\n\nLOOTH. The same as 9 Loo,\n\nLOOT. A thin ohlong square board fixed to staff or handle, used in boiling   brine to remove the scum.\n\nLOOVER. An opening at the of a dove-cote. North. See Lover (2). top\n\nLOOVEYNG. Praise honour.\n\nLOOVEYD. Praised. Ritson.\n\nThat was a feyre tokenynge\n\nMS:\n\nOf pees and of looveyng.\n\nLOOZE.\n\nLOP. (1) A fea. North. (A-s) Ys joy y-now 80 ye your lyggys streyne, Ye lade longe-sydyde a8 a loppe.\n\npig-stye. West. Laughed. At hym ful fast thei MS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 53.\n\nMS. Fairfar 16.\n\nThe faggot wood of a tree.\n\n- 2) To lounge about. Kent. To loosely to down, or Var. dial. lollop hang droop. hang\n\nLeapt.\n\nAlso, to It seems to be\n\nAs sone as the 'chylde had spoke,\n\nsubst. in the second example.\n\nThe fende ynto hym was lope.\n\nTyme goth fast, it Is full lyght of And in abydyng men seyn ther lyghte lope, hope.\n\nUS. Harl. 1701, f. 40.\n\nMS. Rawl Poet. 118.\n\nLOPEN. Leapt. Sec the Sevyn Sages, 739. Whan thy mouthe with shryfte ys opun Deth and synne are bothe oute lopun.\n\nLOP-EARED. Iaving pendulous ears like hound Var. dial. long\n\nMS. Harl. 1701, f. 79.\n\nThe portar set the yatys opon,\n\nCantab Ff. ii. 38, f. 108.\n\nAnd with that Befyse ys owt lopon.\n\nAnoon was al that feire gederynge Lopen undir oure lordes wynge.\n\nSythen he ys lopen on hys stede,\n\nMS: Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 154. LOPE-STAFF. leaping-staff: lope-staffe wherewith men leape ditches, Cotgrave:\n\nHe with hym Harrawde dud lede.\n\nLOPIRD.   Coagulated. Still iu use.  See Forby, Brockett, Grose, Kennett, &amp;c.\n\nBot wlatesome glcte and lopird blcde\n\nThare dwelled a\n\nThare he fande none other fode,\n\nLincoln A.l.\n\nman\n\nAnd In myrke donjowne, in fowle stede of corrupcyowne,\n\nBot wlatsome glette and lopyrd blode.\n\nWhare he had no fode,\n\nLOP-LOACH. The leech used by surgeons for drawing blood. North.\n\nLOPPING. Lame. Dorset.\n\nLOPLOLLY. A lazy fellow . West.\n\nLOP-SIDED. Var. dial\n\nLOPUSTER A lobster:\n\nLOP-START. The stoat. East . It is mentioned in Harrison's England, P. 230.\n\nLOPWEBBE. 5 web. (4-s) II spider'\n\nAs a lopwebbe fileth fome and gnattis, Taken and suffren gret files go.\n\nLORD. (1) A title of given to monks and persons of superior rank. (4.-s) honour\n\nOccleve, MS Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 267. LOQUINTUE Eloquent. Weber\n\n(2) Lord have mercy upon u8 was formerly the inscription on houses infected with tbe plague. Lord have mercy upon me, &amp; disease thus   mentioned in the   Nomenclator, the small guts, which the homelier sort of phisicians doe call, Lorde have mercy upon me-\n\nLORDEYN\\_ See Fever-Lurden. The fever, idleness; Craven Glossary, p. 304. trow he was infecte certeyn\n\nMS. Rawl. C.86, XV. Cent.\n\nLORD-OF-MISRULE. The person who prcsided orer the Christmas revels, by no means an uuimportant personage in the olden times. He began his rule on Al-hallow eve and con tinued it till Candlemas See a list of expences,   dated in 1552, in Kempe's Manuscripts, pp. 44-54. Tor further information on the subject, see Brand, i. 272 ; Arcl xviii, 313-335 Hawkins' Engl. Dram. iii. 156; Strutt, ii. 200 Lilly's Sixe Court Comedies, Loseley\n\nLORD-FEST. Excessively lordly. LORDINGS. Sirs masters. (A.-s) Itis often used hy later writers in contempt.\n\nLORDS-AND-LADIES See Bulls-and-Cows LORDSHIP . Supreme poner. (A.-s)\n\nLORDS-ROOM. The stage-box in theatre was formerly so called. Jonson.\n\nLORD-SIZE .\n\nLORDSWYK A traitor. Ritson.\n\nThe kyng seld, Take me thy tayle,\n\nLORE. (1) Knowledge ; doctrine advice. ( A.-S) (2) Lost. Still in use in Somerset.\n\nFor my hors wolde not the fayle,\n\nA peny that thou lore.\n\nLOREFADYR teacher. Loremastir, Dial. Creat. Moral. p. 243.\n\nHere lorefadyr ys thedevyl. MS. Harl. 1701, f 24, LOREINE. A rcin. Sce Lannfal , 888\n\nOf al men they do most evyl,\n\nHys loreine lemyd alle with pride, Stede and armure alle was blake\n\nMS. Harl. 2252, f. 104\n\nLOREL. bad worthless fellow. A-N) Lorels Holinshed, Chron.  Ireland, P. 93 Cocke Lorel generic title for a very great rascal. lorrels; Harman, 1567. den, Lasy\n\nA-N:) LORER. The laurel-tree. Chaucer.\n\nLOREMAR bit-maker. Palsgrave. Lori . mers or bit-makers; Harrison; p. 97. LORENGE. Iron.\n\nThís Daphne into a lorer tre\n\nWas turnid, whiche is ever grene.\n\nAnd plaunted trees that wcre to prelse,",
